# Microsoft vendor attributes (RFC 2439 / RFC 2548 / RFC 2759).
#
# The `radius` package ships the RFC dictionaries but not Microsoft's, and these
# are the attributes a MikroTik PPPoE server negotiates by default — so without
# this file a RADIUS server answers every login with Access-Reject, and the reason
# is a silently unknown attribute rather than a wrong password.
#
# **Attribute numbers are shared by direction**, which is the detail that trips
# everyone up once:
#
#   11  MS-CHAP-Challenge   (authenticator → peer, sent before the peer's response)
#       MS-CHAP2-Verify     (peer → authenticator, in an Accept)
#   25  MS-CHAP2-Response   (peer → authenticator, in a request)
#       MS-CHAP2-Success    (authenticator → peer, in an Accept)
#
# There is no "MS-CHAP2-Challenge" attribute. A server sends its challenge as
# MS-CHAP-Challenge (11) and its mutual-authentication reply as MS-CHAP2-Success
# (25); inventing the name is a common reason a hand-rolled MS-CHAPv2 server
# rejects every login.
#
# ## Both names, one entry, and why that matters
#
# The parser keeps one entry per attribute id and one name per entry. Two names for
# one id alias the *same* entry, so the type declared last wins for both. That
# forces two consequences this file is arranged around:
#
#   - **Decoding must produce the request-side names**, because those are what a
#     peer sends us and what the auth handler reads. So MS-CHAP2-Success and
#     MS-CHAP2-Verify are declared *before* MS-CHAP-Challenge and MS-CHAP2-Response.
#   - **The shared type is `octets`**, so the Access-Accept's MS-CHAP2-Success must
#     be attached as a raw Buffer rather than as a JS string. A Buffer is written
#     verbatim by the codec, so the wire bytes are right regardless, and the
#     alternative — typing the shared entry `string` for Success's sake — would
#     change the type decoding reports for the 25-octet response and corrupt the
#     parse. `radius/auth.ts` encodes it as a Buffer and says why.
#
# Deliberately typed `octets`, never `encrypt=1`. `encrypt=1` tells the package to
# run the RADIUS User-Password cipher (MD5 of the secret chained with the request
# authenticator) over the value, which is a completely different algorithm from
# what a CHAP response is. Applying it would "decrypt" the challenge into nonsense
# and make every CHAP login fail for a reason that looks like a wrong password. A
# CHAP response is transmitted in the clear, like PAP; the security comes from the
# challenge it answers, not from the transport.

VENDOR Microsoft 311

BEGIN-VENDOR Microsoft

# RFC 2759 §8.10 — the peer challenge carried in a Failure packet
VENDORATTR 311 MS-Peer-Challenge 16 octets

# --- reply side: declared first so they do not win the decode name ---

# RFC 2759 §8.7 — the server's mutual-authentication reply. Attached as a Buffer;
# see the note above.
VENDORATTR 311 MS-CHAP2-Success 25 octets

# RFC 2759 §8.8 — the server's verification of the peer
VENDORATTR 311 MS-CHAP2-Verify 11 octets

# --- request side: declared last so decoding produces these names ---

# RFC 2548 — the authenticator's challenge
VENDORATTR 311 MS-CHAP-Challenge 11 octets

# RFC 2759 §4 — the peer's response
VENDORATTR 311 MS-CHAP2-Response 25 octets

# RFC 2439 — MS-CHAP v1's response, still offered alongside v2
VENDORATTR 311 MS-CHAP-Response 1 octets

END-VENDOR Microsoft